Closes #2858.

> **Builds on #2860** (Define New Bullet… and Change List Level ›, #2786), which adds the menu this changes. #2860's branch is merged in (now at `af0f7a67`, with the latest `main`), so until #2860 merges the diff here includes its commits. **This PR is only `496fa09` and `e680f5a`**: compared with #2860's branch it changes just `crates/ui-egui/src/ribbon.rs` and `docs/ui-parity.md`.

## What changes

Home › Paragraph › Bullets › **Change List Level ›** was nine text items, "Level 1" to "Level 9". Each one is now a small drawn preview of that level of the caret's list:

- a bordered tile with the level's own bullet or number (`•`, `○`, `▪`… or `I.`, `A.`, `1.`, `a.`…) at its aligned-at position, then a grey line from its text position, so the tiles step in as the levels deepen;
- the label is laid out through `previews::glyph`, like document text, so a Symbol/Wingdings bullet shows the character the page will;
- the paragraph's current level is highlighted, as galleries are;
- the level positions are scaled so the deepest level still has room for its line; centred and right-aligned labels sit around their aligned-at position;
- the tile uses the gallery page tint, so it isn't the brightest thing on a dark interface;
- the label is fitted to its tile whatever size the level gives it (`previews::glyph` now honours `chr.size`, from #2860), so a large bullet isn't clipped.

A click still runs `para.listLevel {"level": n}`. The tiles read the paragraph's own list, the one `para.listLevel` changes, matching #2860's gating.

**Accessibility:** each tile is named "Level N" and reports whether it is selected, so tests and screen readers still find it.

No new strings (it reuses "Level"), no asset files: everything is drawn in code. Nothing is taken from Word beyond the feature's name and what it does.

